---
title: Work with existing projects and files
description: Run native project tasks, add files, and review registered file changes.
section: How-to guides
order: 3
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Use an existing project with Rivet by opening a file inside it and running `:RivetTask`. Install the project's native tools first. Discovery recognizes CMake, Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, and configured Lua projects; it creates no `.rivet.json`. The [project model](/docs/project-model/) explains which operations require that manifest.

## Build and run an existing CMake project

1. Run `:RivetTask configure` and choose a configure preset. Without preset files, Rivet offers Debug and Release with Ninja under `build/rivet/debug` and `build/rivet/release`.
2. Run `:RivetBuildTarget` to select a target discovered through CMake's File API, then `:RivetTask build` or `:RivetTask test`.
3. Run `:RivetRunTarget` to select an executable, then `:RivetTask run`. Rivet resolves the configured artifact, including custom output names and directories.

The CLI equivalent is:

```sh
rivet configure --root .
rivet targets --root .
rivet run --root . --target viewer
```

Replace `viewer` with a listed executable. A fresh project's `rivet run --root .` configures, discovers, builds, and runs automatically when there is one executable. Choose a target when there are several.

Native build/test presets control the task when present. A multi-config project needs a selected configuration, typically from its build preset. Build directories and executable artifacts must stay inside the project; in-source builds and symlink paths are rejected. To use cache overrides in a project without presets, create native presets first. See [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/).

Configuration executes the project's CMake scripts, writes a File API query in the build directory, and copies the compilation database to `.cache/rivet`. For a custom `.clangd`, set `CompileFlags.CompilationDatabase` to `.cache/rivet` to follow the selected build.

## Run other native projects

Open a file under the native project root and choose a task with `:RivetTask`. Recognized markers are `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, `Package.swift`, `.luarc.json`, `.luacheckrc`, and `.stylua.toml`. Use `--root PATH` to select a CLI root explicitly.

Follow the [Python](/docs/python/), [Rust](/docs/rust/), [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/), or [Lua](/docs/lua/) reference for task requirements. Native tools retain their normal dependency and lockfile behavior.

For external Lua projects, syntax checks discover `.lua` files under the root. Run expects `main.lua`; Test expects `tests/test.lua`. The runtime defaults to ordinary Lua. A local executable override must accept Lua-style invocation; use a generated Lua project for a recorded Neovim runtime and plugin tests.

## Add files without adopting the build

Run `:RivetFile`, select the language/template, and review the paths. Apply with `c`, then register or import the new files through your native build workflow. The operation leaves build registration to you and creates no Rivet manifest.

In an ordinary folder without a project marker, give the CLI an explicit root:

```sh
rivet file scripts/prepare.lua --language lua --kind script --root . --preview
rivet file scripts/prepare.lua --language lua --kind script --root .
```

## Move or remove a registered file

This operation supports C/C++ target files and supplemental files in another language recorded in `.rivet.json`. Use native tools to move or remove a Python, Rust, Swift, or Lua project's own source files. Run `:RivetFiles` and choose a project-relative move, rename, or removal. Inspect the source and manifest changes; for C/C++, also inspect regenerated source lists. Press `c` to apply or `q` to cancel.

For example, after registering `src/core/extra.cpp`:

```sh
rivet move src/core/extra.cpp src/core/detail.cpp --preview
rivet move src/core/extra.cpp src/core/detail.cpp
rivet remove src/core/detail.cpp --preview
```

Omit `--preview` on the last command to remove the file. Keep the extension when moving. Moves preserve bytes and module identity, so update affected `#include` paths and identifiers yourself. Avoid leaving a target without a compiled source; removing a referenced module is refused.

Save affected Neovim buffers before applying. Rivet rechecks the reviewed snapshots, refuses collisions or changed files, and rolls back committed contents if a write fails. After applying, open buffers follow moved paths, removed-file buffers close, and clean buffers for rewritten files refresh. Build and test afterward.

## Rename a C++ module's identity

Run `:RivetModuleRename`, or preview a CLI rename:

```sh
rivet module-rename simulation.core simulation.work --preview
```

Inspect declarations and imports, then repeat without `--preview` to apply. This updates direct named-module declarations, implementation declarations, and imports in registered project files. The scan skips comments and string literals; handle dependencies, macros, partitions, and unregistered files yourself. Build and test after the rename.
